INSERT INTO trick_tutorials (name, difficulty, category, description, tips) VALUES
('Ollie','beginner','flatground',
'Back foot on the tail, front foot just behind the bolts. Crouch, snap the tail down hard, and as it hits, drag the outside edge of your front foot up toward the nose. Level the board out at the peak by pushing your front foot forward, then bend your knees to absorb the landing.',
ARRAY['Snap the tail — don''t push it. It''s a pop, not a press.','Your front foot slide is what gets you height, not jumping harder.','Practice stationary first, then rolling slow. Rolling is actually easier.','Look where you''re going, not at your board.','Shoulders stay square over the board or you''ll drift off.']),

('Manual','beginner','balance',
'Back foot on the tail, front foot over the front bolts. Lean back gently until the front wheels lift, then hold. The balance point is smaller than you think — small ankle adjustments, not big body swings.',
ARRAY['Arms out for balance, no shame in it.','Find the balance point rolling slow, not standing still.','Eyes up and forward — looking down kills your balance.','If you feel yourself going over, tap the nose down and reset.','Start on flat ground before trying manual pads.']),

('Pop Shove-it','beginner','flatground',
'Back foot in the pocket of the tail, angled slightly. Front foot behind the bolts. Pop the tail while scooping backward with your back foot, then lift both feet and let the board spin 180 under you. Catch it with your front foot.',
ARRAY['The scoop is backward, not down. Down is an ollie.','Jump straight up — don''t rotate your body with the board.','If the board flips, you''re scooping at an angle instead of flat.','Land on the bolts with both feet at once.','Toe drag is common — pick your front foot up higher.']),

('Kickflip','beginner','flip',
'Feet like an ollie but front foot angled slightly, heel hanging off a touch. Pop the tail, slide your front foot up toward the nose pocket, then flick out at 45 degrees off the corner. Let the board flip, spot the grip tape, catch with your back foot then front.',
ARRAY['The flick is out and up, not down. Downward flick makes it flip lopsided.','Wait to see grip tape before you catch — catching early kills the flip.','Jump up, not forward. Rolling away comes with commitment.','If it''s only half flipping, flick harder off the corner.','If it''s flipping behind you, your front foot is too far back.']),

('Heelflip','beginner','flip',
'Front foot angled with toes hanging off the heel side, back foot on the tail. Pop, slide your front foot forward and diagonally, then flick off your heel toward the nose. The board flips toward you — opposite of a kickflip.',
ARRAY['Flick with the heel, not the whole foot rolling over.','Keep your shoulders square or it turns into a varial.','Feet come apart wider than a kickflip on the catch.','If it flips too fast and hits your shin, ease off the flick.','Land over the bolts, not behind them.']),

('Frontside 180','beginner','rotation',
'Ollie setup. Wind your shoulders backside before you pop, then unwind hard as you snap. Your body leads, the board follows. Turn your head and look over your front shoulder at where you''re landing.',
ARRAY['Wind up first — the pre-rotation does most of the work.','Head turns first, body follows, board comes last.','Scoop the tail slightly to help the board around.','Landing in fakie feels weird at first. Ride it out, don''t bail.','Commit to the full 180 or you''ll land sideways and slip out.']),

('Backside 180','beginner','rotation',
'Ollie setup with shoulders wound frontside. Pop and unwind, turning your head over your back shoulder. Backside is blind — you can''t see the landing until you''re most of the way around, so commit.',
ARRAY['Look over your back shoulder as early as you can.','Scoop the tail a little to bring the board with you.','This one is scarier than frontside because it''s blind. Commit fully.','Keep your weight centered — leaning back throws you off.','Land in fakie with knees bent and roll it out.']),

('50-50 Grind','intermediate','grind',
'Approach parallel to the ledge, roughly a board''s width away. Ollie up, level the board so both trucks land flat on the edge, and keep your weight centered directly over the ledge. Ride it out and ollie off the end.',
ARRAY['Speed is your friend. Too slow and you''ll stick and stop dead.','Weight centered — leaning in or out both end badly.','Wax the ledge if it''s grabby. Everyone does it.','Look at the end of the ledge, not at your feet.','Ollie out the end instead of just rolling off.']),

('Boardslide','intermediate','slide',
'Approach at a slight angle, ollie so your front trucks clear the rail, and turn 90 degrees so the board lands underfoot across the obstacle. Weight over your front foot, shoulders parallel to the rail. Turn back out at the end.',
ARRAY['Get the front trucks all the way over before turning.','Shoulders parallel to the rail keeps you from over-rotating.','Weight slightly forward — leaning back sends the board out.','Start on a low, waxed ledge before touching a real rail.','Look at the end of the obstacle, not down at the rail.']),

('5-0 Grind','intermediate','grind',
'A 50-50 but only the back truck grinds — hold a manual position on the ledge. Ollie up, land back truck on the edge, keep the nose lifted, and stay centered. Harder than 50-50 because you''re balancing while grinding.',
ARRAY['Get comfortable with manuals first — this is a manual on a ledge.','Weight stays over your back truck, not behind it.','Don''t lift the nose too high or you''ll loop out.','Speed carries you through. Slow means sticking.','Level out before you ollie off the end.']),

('Tailslide','intermediate','slide',
'Approach parallel, frontside 90 with the tail landing on the ledge. Weight over the tail, front foot guiding. Keep shoulders open toward the direction you''re sliding, then turn out at the end.',
ARRAY['Frontside 90 first, then let the tail settle onto the ledge.','Weight on the tail — too much front foot and you''ll slip out.','Wax matters a lot on this one.','Keep your knees bent and stay low.','Turn the shoulders back out to exit clean.']),

('Nollie','intermediate','flatground',
'Front foot on the nose, back foot around the middle of the board. Pop off the nose and slide your back foot toward the tail. Same mechanics as an ollie, reversed — feels awkward until it doesn''t.',
ARRAY['It''s an ollie backwards. Same pop, same slide, different foot.','Pop off the very tip of the nose for the best snap.','Your back foot does the leveling out.','Practice nollie manuals to get used to the weight shift.','Start stationary, then slow roll.']),

('Varial Kickflip','intermediate','flip',
'Kickflip front foot, pop shove-it back foot. Pop and scoop backward while flicking the kickflip. The board flips and rotates 180 at the same time. Catch it once it''s fully around.',
ARRAY['Scoop and flick happen together, not one after the other.','If it lands primo, your flick is beating the scoop.','Keep your feet apart on the catch — this one loves to land sideways.','Jump straight up, not backward with the scoop.','Watch for the grip tape and full rotation before catching.']),

('Tre Flip (360 Flip)','advanced','flip',
'Back foot deep in the tail pocket, angled hard. Front foot low and angled for the flick. Pop and scoop hard backward while flicking out at 45 degrees. The board does a full 360 shove and a kickflip together. This one takes months. That''s normal.',
ARRAY['The scoop is the hard part. Scoop harder than feels reasonable.','Back foot needs to be deep in the pocket, well hung off the toe side.','Everyone lands this primo for weeks first. Keep going.','Jump up and slightly back to give the board room.','Spot the grip and catch with your back foot first.']),

('Smith Grind','advanced','grind',
'Back truck grinds while the front truck hangs off the near side, board angled down into the obstacle. Requires committing your weight over the back truck while keeping the nose turned in.',
ARRAY['Weight is over the back truck the whole time.','Nose stays turned into the obstacle, not away.','Speed carries you. Hesitation gets you stuck.','Wax helps a lot when you''re learning it.','Level out before you pop off the end.'])
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

